--TEST--
Core\Jwe::decrypt refuses an empty ring and a password key sharing a ring, and Jwe\Key::shared names the key length on both sides — `rule:security/jwe-compact-subset`, `rule:security/protocol-roster`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// A ring is tried in order, which is what makes rotation cheap for the three
// cheap algorithms. A password is the expensive one: every try is a full
// PBKDF2, so a ring whoever sent the token can lengthen is the iteration
// ceiling defeated by multiplying it. The member refuses the shape rather than
// capping the ring, because a cap is a number somebody would have to pick.
secret bytes $key = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
string $token = Core\Jwe::encrypt("id=42", Core\Jwe\Key::shared($key));

array<Core\Jwe\Key> $empty = [];
try {
    tainted string $nothing = Core\Jwe::decrypt($token, $empty);
    echo "an empty ring opened it", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $bug) {
    echo $bug->message, "\n";
}

try {
    tainted string $mixed = Core\Jwe::decrypt($token, [
        Core\Jwe\Key::shared($key),
        Core\Jwe\Key::password("correct horse battery staple"),
    ]);
    echo "a mixed ring opened it", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $bug) {
    echo $bug->message, "\n";
}

// The refusal is about the ring and not about the password: the same key alone
// is a ring this member takes, and it opens the token it sealed.
string $sealed = Core\Jwe::encrypt("id=42", Core\Jwe\Key::password("correct horse battery staple"));
tainted string $alone = Core\Jwe::decrypt($sealed, [Core\Jwe\Key::password("correct horse battery staple")]);
echo $alone == "id=42" ? "a password ring of one" : "a password ring of one failed", "\n";

// And a ring of three shared keys is fine, because none of them costs a
// derivation: the newest opens what it sealed and the retired ones are still
// tried for what they sealed.
secret bytes $retired = Core\Crypto::generateKey();
string $old = Core\Jwe::encrypt("id=7", Core\Jwe\Key::shared($retired));
tainted string $rotated = Core\Jwe::decrypt($old, [
    Core\Jwe\Key::shared($key),
    Core\Jwe\Key::shared($retired),
]);
echo $rotated == "id=7" ? "a retired key still opens" : "a retired key did not open", "\n";

// The length bound, on both sides: 32 octets is a key and 31 is a `bytes` that
// was never one, refused where it is built rather than where it is used. The
// message names the length wanted and never the octets given, because a key
// does not belong in a log.
Core\Jwe\Key $exact = Core\Jwe\Key::shared(
    Core\Encoding::fromHex("0e23743411f4f8a11b9b229ce598e431adb7994963d03c87e20dd6781939ef96")
);
echo "32 octets is a key", "\n";
try {
    Core\Jwe\Key $short = Core\Jwe\Key::shared(
        Core\Encoding::fromHex("0e23743411f4f8a11b9b229ce598e431adb7994963d03c87e20dd6781939ef")
    );
    echo "31 octets is a key", "\n";
} catch (LogicError $bug) {
    echo $bug->message, "\n";
}
--EXPECT--
Core\Jwe::decrypt(): $keys is empty, and a key ring needs at least one key — one of Core\Jwe\Key's four statics answers one.
Core\Jwe::decrypt(): $keys holds a key built by Core\Jwe\Key::password beside another key, and a password ring holds exactly one. Every try costs a full derivation, so a ring whoever sent the token can lengthen is the iteration ceiling defeated one layer up.
a password ring of one
a retired key still opens
32 octets is a key
Core\Jwe\Key::shared(): $key is 31 octets, and a key is 32 — Core\Crypto::generateKey() answers one of the right length. The value is not quoted here, because a key does not belong in a log.
